Pouch fcntl locks and flock over native SYS_FILE_LOCK 137 (B-3h4; operator
votes 34 and 35, ARCH 9.6.11). Its definitions live in _pouch_lock.h, which
only the lock adapters include.

fcntl F_SETLK, F_SETLKW and F_GETLK name the Proc as the lock's owner, which
is POSIX's descriptor table: any close of the file by the Proc drops its
locks, threads share them, and a child does not inherit them. F_OFD_SETLK,
F_OFD_SETLKW and F_OFD_GETLK name the open file, and l_pid must be 0
(EINVAL). The range is Linux's (flock64_to_posix_lock): l_whence SEEK_SET,
SEEK_CUR (the cursor; 0 on a pipe) or SEEK_END (fstat's size, so the cursor never moves);
a negative l_len locks the bytes before l_start; l_len 0 runs to the end of
the file; past LLONG_MAX is EOVERFLOW and below 0 EINVAL. The refusals come
in Linux's order: whence, the range, the type, the access mode, then an OFD
request's l_pid. A refusal the adapter makes before the kernel sees the
request first asks the kernel about the descriptor, so a closed one answers
EBADF, as Linux does; flock reads its op first, as Linux does. F_SETLKW waits in the kernel and is a cancellation
point. F_GETLK reports a conflicting lock's type and range with l_whence
SEEK_SET and l_pid -1, Linux's answer for an OFD lock (the server keeps no
process ids); a free range changes only l_type, to F_UNLCK. l_type maps by
name, not by value. A struct flock the caller cannot read or write faults in
the caller, where Linux answers EFAULT.

flock (it was __NR_flock 0xFFFF -> ENOSYS) is a whole-file lock owned by the
open file, which is how Linux's NFS client serves it: flock and fcntl locks
on one file conflict, LOCK_EX needs a descriptor open for writing (EBADF,
flock(2) NOTES), and LOCK_NB's conflict is EWOULDBLOCK.

Only a Dev that serves locks takes them (9P files, Stratum's lock table);
every other file answers ENOLCK, as NFS without lockd does. A native
caller's wait ends only on a grant or a kill until native waits take caught
notes (ARCH 8.8.3), so F_SETLKW's EINTR is not reachable yet. Tagged Pouch
sockets answer ENOSYS (B-3k gives them kernel descriptors).

--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/internal/_pouch_lock.h
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+/* Lock adapters only, so a checker of the descriptor header need not mock these. */
+#ifndef _POUCH_LOCK_H
+#define _POUCH_LOCK_H
+#include <errno.h>
+#include "syscall.h"
+#include "_pouch_descriptor.h"
+
+/* SYS_thyla_file_lock (kernel syscall.h SYS_FILE_LOCK and its op word): a
+ * byte-range lock the file's server holds. The owner is the open file, or
+ * with PROC the Proc's descriptor table. len 0 runs to the end of the file.
+ * A TEST writes struct pouch_file_lock: type UN when the lock would be
+ * granted, else the first conflicting lock (pid always -1). */
+#define SYS_thyla_file_lock          137
+#define POUCH_FILE_LOCK_RD           0
+#define POUCH_FILE_LOCK_WR           1
+#define POUCH_FILE_LOCK_UN           2
+#define POUCH_FILE_LOCK_TYPE_MASK    0xf
+#define POUCH_FILE_LOCK_WAIT         0x10
+#define POUCH_FILE_LOCK_TEST         0x20
+#define POUCH_FILE_LOCK_PROC         0x40
+#define POUCH_FILE_LOCK_FLAGS_ALL    (POUCH_FILE_LOCK_WAIT | POUCH_FILE_LOCK_TEST | \
+                                      POUCH_FILE_LOCK_PROC)
+struct pouch_file_lock {
+    unsigned char type;
+    unsigned char _pad0[7];
+    unsigned long long start;
+    unsigned long long len;
+    int pid;
+    unsigned _pad1;
+};
+
+/* A lock request the adapter refuses before the kernel sees it. Linux has
+ * already answered a closed descriptor EBADF by then, so ask the kernel. */
+static inline int pouch_lock_refuse(int fd, int e)
+{
+    if (__syscall1(SYS_thyla_handle_flags_get, fd) == -EBADF) e = EBADF;
+    errno = e;
+    return -1;
+}
+#endif
